WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:03.440
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:09.980
Have you ever had a moment where you took
the perfect photo, but upon closer inspection,

00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:13.839
there was this one annoying thing that ruined
the whole picture?

00:00:13.839 --> 00:00:19.550
Well, why not just take a learning algorithm
to erase those cracks in the facade of a building,

00:00:19.550 --> 00:00:21.480
or a photobombing sheep?

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:27.260
Or, to even reimagine ourselves with different
eye colors, we can try one of the many research

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:32.029
works that are capable of something that we
call image inpainting.

00:00:32.029 --> 00:00:37.460
What you see here is the legendary PatchMatch
algorithm at work, which, believe it or not,

00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:41.239
is a handcrafted technique from more than
10 years ago.

00:00:41.239 --> 00:00:47.010
Later, scientists at NVIDIA published a more
modern inpainter that uses a learning-based

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:52.749
algorithm to do this more reliably, and for
a greater variety of images.

00:00:52.749 --> 00:00:57.359
These all work really well, but the common
denominator for these techniques is that they

00:00:57.359 --> 00:01:00.469
all work on inpainting still images.

00:01:00.469 --> 00:01:02.829
Would this be a possibility for video?

00:01:02.829 --> 00:01:07.190
Like, removing a moving object or person from
a video?

00:01:07.190 --> 00:01:10.300
Is this possible, or is it science fiction?

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:14.370
Let’s see if these learning-based techniques
can really do more.

00:01:14.370 --> 00:01:19.750
And now, hold on to your papers, because this
new work can really perform proper inpainting

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:20.880
for video.

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:24.610
Let’s give it a try by highlighting this
human.

00:01:24.610 --> 00:01:30.160
And pro tip: also highlight the shadowy region
for inpainting to make sure that not only

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:34.410
the human, but its silhouette also disappears
from the footage.

00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:36.270
And, look!

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:37.270
Wow.

00:01:37.270 --> 00:01:40.640
Let’s look at some other examples.

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:45.500
Now that’s really something because video
is much more difficult due to the requirement

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:50.680
of temporal coherence, which means that it’s
not nearly enough if the images are inpainted

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:56.260
really well individually, they also have to
look good if we weave them together into a

00:01:56.260 --> 00:01:57.260
video.

00:01:57.260 --> 00:02:00.330
You will hear and see more about this in a
moment.

00:02:00.330 --> 00:02:05.240
Not only that, but if we highlight a person,
this person not only needs to be inpainted,

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:10.399
but we also have to track the boundaries of
this person throughout the footage and then

00:02:10.399 --> 00:02:12.530
inpaint a moving region.

00:02:12.530 --> 00:02:16.239
We get some help with that, which I will also
talk about in a moment.

00:02:16.239 --> 00:02:22.310
Now, as you see here, these all work extremely
well, and believe it or not, you have seen

00:02:22.310 --> 00:02:27.599
nothing yet, because so far, another common
denominator in these examples was that we

00:02:27.599 --> 00:02:30.280
highlighted regions inside the video.

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:31.900
But that’s not all.

00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:36.349
If you have been holding on to your papers
so far, now squeeze that paper, because we

00:02:36.349 --> 00:02:42.879
can also go outside, and expand our video
spatially with even more content.

00:02:42.879 --> 00:02:45.909
This one is very short so I will keep looping
it.

00:02:45.909 --> 00:02:46.930
Are you ready?

00:02:46.930 --> 00:02:48.329
Let’s go.

00:02:48.329 --> 00:02:49.329
Wow!

00:02:49.329 --> 00:02:50.420
My goodness!

00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:55.390
The information from inside of the video frames
is reused to infer what should be around the

00:02:55.390 --> 00:02:59.650
video frame, and all this in a temporally
coherent manner.

00:02:59.650 --> 00:03:05.219
Now, of course, this is not the first technique
to perform this, so let’s see how it compares

00:03:05.219 --> 00:03:09.629
to the competition by erasing this bear from
the video footage.

00:03:09.629 --> 00:03:14.499
The remnants of the bear are visible with
a wide selection of previously published techniques

00:03:14.499 --> 00:03:16.720
from the last few years.

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:21.470
This is true even for these four methods from
last year.

00:03:21.470 --> 00:03:29.120
And, let’s see how this new method did one
the same case.

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:34.709
Yup, very good, not perfect, we still see
some flickering.

00:03:34.709 --> 00:03:39.170
This is the temporal coherence example, or
the lack thereof that I have promised earlier.

00:03:39.170 --> 00:03:43.529
But now, let’s look at this example with
the BMX rider.

00:03:43.529 --> 00:03:51.060
We see similar performance with the previous
techniques, and now, let’s have a look at

00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:52.060
the new one.

00:03:52.060 --> 00:03:54.349
Now that’s what I’m talking about!

00:03:54.349 --> 00:03:59.569
Not a trace left from this person, the only
clue that we get in reconstructing what went

00:03:59.569 --> 00:04:01.719
down here is the camera movement.

00:04:01.719 --> 00:04:05.840
It truly feels like we are living in a science
fiction world.

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:07.889
What a time to be alive!

00:04:07.889 --> 00:04:12.549
Now these were the qualitative results, and
now, let’s have a look at the quantitative

00:04:12.549 --> 00:04:13.549
results.

00:04:13.549 --> 00:04:18.120
In other words, we saw the videos, now let’s
see what the numbers say.

00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:24.240
We could talk all day about the peak signal
to noise ratios or structural similarity or

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:28.550
other ways to measure how good these techniques
are, but you will see in a moment that it

00:04:28.550 --> 00:04:31.139
is completely unnecessary.

00:04:31.139 --> 00:04:32.150
Why is that?

00:04:32.150 --> 00:04:38.150
Well, you see here that the second best results
are underscored and highlighted with blue.

00:04:38.150 --> 00:04:42.870
As you see, there is plenty of competition,
as the blues are all over the place.

00:04:42.870 --> 00:04:47.479
But there is no competition at all for the
first place, because this new method smokes

00:04:47.479 --> 00:04:50.479
the competition in every category.

00:04:50.479 --> 00:04:55.740
This was measured on a dataset by the name
Densely Annotated Video Segmentation, DAVIS

00:04:55.740 --> 00:05:02.449
in short, this contains 150 video sequences
and it is annotated, which means that many

00:05:02.449 --> 00:05:07.159
of the objects are highlighted throughout
this video, so for the cases in this dataset,

00:05:07.159 --> 00:05:10.400
we don’t have to deal with the tracking
ourselves.

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:15.680
I am truly out of ideas as to what I should
wish for two more papers down the line.

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:21.050
Maybe not only removing the tennis player,
but putting myself in there as a proxy?

00:05:21.050 --> 00:05:26.509
We can already grab a controller and play
as if we were real characters in real broadcast

00:05:26.509 --> 00:05:29.030
footage, so who really knows.

00:05:29.030 --> 00:05:30.629
Anything is possible.

00:05:30.629 --> 00:05:34.789
Let me know in the comments what you have
in mind for potential applications and what

00:05:34.789 --> 00:06:13.319
you would be excited to see two more papers
down 

00:06:13.319 --> 00:06:22.909
the line!

00:06:22.909 --> 00:06:31.450
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

